@* The hosts' family on the Architecture page: the libraries a demo's program starts, and the accounts beside them. *@
<Band Id="hosts" Kicker="src/hosts · the hosts">
    <h3>Libraries a few lines of yours start.</h3>
    <p class="lede">A host maps the agent's stream to one protocol and holds no behaviour of its own. It ships as a library, and your program names the catalogue and starts it.</p>
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.cli</code></strong> A chat in the terminal, interactive or one prompt and out.</li>
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.acp</code></strong> The agent inside JetBrains Rider's AI chat, over the Agent Client Protocol on stdio.</li>
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.api</code></strong> The HTTP API: sessions, answers streamed as server-sent events, the workspace and git routes, the admin screens. It serves a site's web app from the same origin.</li>
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.api.contracts</code></strong> The API's replies and bodies as records, so a client reads what the server writes.</li>
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.api.evals</code></strong> The eval studio on the API: the runner started as a child process, the stores, the recorders, the reports and their admin routes. A program opts in.</li>
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.mcp</code></strong> Hands the agent's tools to any MCP client, which brings its own model, over stdio or HTTP.</li>
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.accounts</code></strong> Sign-in, registration and profiles on ASP.NET Core Identity, a tenant on every account from the host name, EF Core on SQL Server, with social logins, passkeys and a second factor.</li>
        <li><strong><code>louis-agent.accounts.contracts</code></strong> The records the account routes send and read.</li>
    </ul>
</Band>
